      "id": 1,
      "text": "Jetmore was originally laid out as Buckner in 1879 before being renamed.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jetmore%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town was renamed Jetmore in 1880 to honor Colonel Abraham Buckles Jetmore, a Topeka lawyer who secured a railroad line and made the town the county seat.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://legendsofkansas.com/jetmore-kansas/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Buckner Creek, a tributary of the Pawnee River, winds through Jetmore, giving the town a notable waterway.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jetmore%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The bedrock beneath Jetmore consists of Greenhorn Limestone and Graneros Shale, typical of the region’s geology.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jetmore%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1972, a tornado struck Jetmore, and the town was also the site where the Hunt Rotary Aeroplane, an early helicopter, was built and tested.",
